.TH "OSMIUM-EXTRACT" "1" "1.19.1" "" ""
.SH NAME
osmium\-extract \- create geographical extracts from an OSM file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]osmium extract\f[R] \-\-config \f[I]CONFIG\-FILE\f[R]
[\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[I]OSM\-FILE\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]osmium extract\f[R] \-\-bbox
\f[I]LEFT\f[R],\f[I]BOTTOM\f[R],\f[I]RIGHT\f[R],\f[I]TOP\f[R]
[\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[I]OSM\-FILE\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]osmium extract\f[R] \-\-polygon \f[I]POLYGON\-FILE\f[R]
[\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[I]OSM\-FILE\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Create geographical extracts from an OSM data file or an OSM history
file.
The region (geographical extent) can be given as a bounding box or as a
(multi)polygon.
.PP
There are three ways of calling this command:
.IP \(bu 2
Specify a config file with the \f[B]\-\-config/\-c\f[R] option.
It can define any number of regions you want to cut out.
See the \f[B]CONFIG FILE\f[R] section for details.
.IP \(bu 2
Specify a bounding box to cut out with the \f[B]\-\-bbox/\-b\f[R]
option.
.IP \(bu 2
Specify a (multi)polygon to cut out with the \f[B]\-\-polygon/\-p\f[R]
option.
.PP
The input file is assumed to be ordered in the usual order: nodes first,
then ways, then relations.
.PP
If the \f[B]\-\-with\-history/\-H\f[R] option is used, the command will
work correctly for history files.
This currently works for the \f[B]complete_ways\f[R] strategy only.
The \f[B]simple\f[R] or \f[B]smart\f[R] strategies do not work with
history files.
A history extract will contain every version of all objects with at
least one version in the region.
Generating a history extract is somewhat slower than a normal data
extract.
.PP
Osmium will make sure that all nodes on the vertices of the boundary of
the region will be in the extract, but nodes that happen to be directly
on the boundary, but between those vertices, might end up in the extract
or not.
In almost all cases this will be good enough, but if you want to make
really sure you got everything, use a small buffer around your region.
.PP
By default no \f[B]bounds\f[R] will be set in the header of the output
file.
Use the \f[B]\-\-set\-bounds\f[R] option if you need this.
.PP
Note that \f[B]osmium extract\f[R] will never clip any OSM objects, ie.
it will not remove node references outside the region from ways or
unused relation members from relations.
This means you might get objects that are not reference\-complete.
It has the advantage that you can use \f[B]osmium merge\f[R] to merge
several extracts without problems.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\-b, \-\-bbox=LONG1,LAT1,LONG2,LAT2
Set the bounding box to cut out.
Can not be used with \f[B]\-\-polygon/\-p\f[R],
\f[B]\-\-config/\-c\f[R], or \f[B]\-\-directory/\-d\f[R].
The coordinates LONG1,LAT1 are from one arbitrary corner, the
coordinates LONG2,LAT2 are from the opposite corner.
.TP
\-c, \-\-config=FILE
Set the name of the config file.
Can not be used with the \f[B]\-\-bbox/\-b\f[R] or
\f[B]\-\-polygon/\-p\f[R] option.
If this is set, the \f[B]\-\-output/\-o\f[R] and
\f[B]\-\-output\-format/\-f\f[R] options are ignored, because they are
set in the config file.
.TP
\-\-clean=ATTR
Clean the attribute (\f[I]version\f[R], \f[I]timestamp\f[R],
\f[I]changeset\f[R], \f[I]uid\f[R], \f[I]user\f[R]), from the data
before writing it out again.
The attribute will be set to 0 (the user will be set to the empty
string).
This option can be given multiple times.
Depending on the output format these attributes might show up as 0 or
not show up at all.
.TP
\-d, \-\-directory=DIRECTORY
Output directory.
Output file names in the config file are relative to this directory.
Overwrites the setting of the same name in the config file.
This option is ignored when the \f[B]\-\-bbox/\-b\f[R] or
\f[B]\-\-polygon/\-p\f[R] options are used, set the output directory and
name with the \f[B]\-\-output/\-o\f[R] option in that case.
.TP
\-H, \-\-with\-history
Specify that the input file is a history file.
The output file(s) will also be history file(s).
.TP
\-p, \-\-polygon=POLYGON_FILE
Set the polygon to cut out based on the contents of the file.
The file has to be a GeoJSON, poly, or OSM file as described in the
\f[B](MULTI)POLYGON FILE FORMATS\f[R] section.
It has to have the right suffix to be detected correctly.
Can not be used with \f[B]\-\-bbox/\-b\f[R], \f[B]\-\-config/\-c\f[R],
or \f[B]\-\-directory/\-d\f[R].
.TP
\-s, \-\-strategy=STRATEGY
Use the given strategy to extract the region.
For possible values and details see the \f[B]STRATEGIES\f[R] section.
Default is \(lqcomplete_ways\(rq.
.TP
\-S, \-\-option=OPTION=VALUE
Set a named option for the strategy.
If needed you can specify this option multiple times to set several
options.
.TP
\-\-set\-bounds
Set the bounds field in the header.
The bounds are set to the bbox or envelope of the polygon specified for
the extract.
Note that strategies other than \(lqsimple\(rq can put nodes outside
those bounds into the output file.
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
\-h, \-\-help
Show usage help.
.TP
\-v, \-\-verbose
Set verbose mode.
The program will output information about what it is doing to STDERR.
.SH INPUT OPTIONS
.TP
\-F, \-\-input\-format=FORMAT
The format of the input file(s).
Can be used to set the input format if it can\(cqt be autodetected from
the file name(s).
This will set the format for all input files, there is no way to set the
format for some input files only.
See \f[B]osmium\-file\-formats\f[R](5) or the libosmium manual for
details.
.SH OUTPUT OPTIONS
.TP
\-f, \-\-output\-format=FORMAT
The format of the output file.
Can be used to set the output file format if it can\(cqt be autodetected
from the output file name.
See \f[B]osmium\-file\-formats\f[R](5) or the libosmium manual for
details.
.TP
\-\-fsync
Call fsync after writing the output file to force flushing buffers to
disk.
.TP
\-\-generator=NAME
The name and version of the program generating the output file.
It will be added to the header of the output file.
Default is \(lq\f[I]osmium/\f[R]\(rq and the version of osmium.
.TP
\-o, \-\-output=FILE
Name of the output file.
Default is `\-' (STDOUT).
.TP
\-O, \-\-overwrite
Allow an existing output file to be overwritten.
Normally \f[B]osmium\f[R] will refuse to write over an existing file.
.TP
\-\-output\-header=OPTION=VALUE
Add output header option.
This command line option can be used multiple times for different
OPTIONs.
See the \f[I]osmium\-output\-headers(5)\f[R] man page for a list of
available header options.
For some commands you can use the special format \(lqOPTION!\(rq (ie.
an exclamation mark after the OPTION and no value set) to set the value
to the same as in the input file.
.SH CONFIG FILE
The config file mainly specifies the file names and the regions of the
extracts that should be created.
.PP
The config file is in JSON format.
The top\-level is an object which contains at least an \(lqextracts\(rq
array.
It can also contain a \(lqdirectory\(rq entry which names the directory
where all the output files will be created:
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqextracts\(dq: [...],
    \(dqdirectory\(dq: \(dq/tmp/\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
The extracts array specifies the extracts that should be created.
Each item in the array is an object with at least a name \(lqoutput\(rq
naming the output file and a region defined in a \(lqbbox\(rq,
\(lqpolygon\(rq or \(lqmultipolygon\(rq name.
An optional \(lqdescription\(rq can be added, it will not be used by the
program but can help with documenting the file contents.
You can add an optional \(lqoutput_format\(rq if the format can not be
detected from the \(lqoutput\(rq file name.
Run \(lqosmium help file\-formats\(rq to get a description of allowed
formats.
.PP
The optional \(lqoutput_header\(rq allows you to set additional OSM file
header settings such as the \(lqgenerator\(rq.
If you set the value of a file header setting to \f[CR]null\f[R], the
output header will be set to the same header from the input file.
.IP
.EX
\(dqextracts\(dq: [
    {
        \(dqoutput\(dq: \(dqhamburg.osm.pbf\(dq,
        \(dqoutput_format\(dq: \(dqpbf\(dq,
        \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqoptional description\(dq,
        \(dqbbox\(dq: ...
    },
    {
        \(dqoutput\(dq: \(dqberlin.osm.pbf\(dq,
        \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqoptional description\(dq,
        \(dqpolygon\(dq: ...
    },
    {
        \(dqoutput\(dq: \(dqmunich.osm.pbf\(dq,
        \(dqoutput_header\(dq: {
            \(dqgenerator\(dq: \(dqMyExtractor/1.0\(dq,
            \(dqosmosis_replication_timestamp\(dq: null
        },
        \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqoptional description\(dq,
        \(dqmultipolygon\(dq: ...
    }
]
.EE
.PP
There are several formats for specifying the regions:
.PP
\f[B]bbox\f[R]:
.PP
A bounding box in one of two formats.
The first is a simple array with four real numbers, the first two
specifying the coordinates of an arbitrary corner, the second two
specifying the coordinates of the opposite corner.
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqoutput\(dq: \(dqmunich.osm.pbf\(dq,
    \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqBounding box specified in array format\(dq,
    \(dqbbox\(dq: [11.35, 48.05, 11.73, 48.25]
}
.EE
.PP
The second format uses an object instead of an array:
.IP
.EX
{
    \(dqoutput\(dq: \(dqdresden.osm.pbf\(dq,
    \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqBounding box specified in object format\(dq,
    \(dqbbox\(dq: {
        \(dqleft\(dq: 13.57,
        \(dqright\(dq: 13.97,
        \(dqtop\(dq: 51.18,
        \(dqbottom\(dq: 50.97
    }
}
.EE
.PP
\f[B]polygon\f[R]:
.PP
A polygon, either specified inline in the config file or read from an
external file.
See the \f[B](MULTI)POLYGON FILE FORMATS\f[R] section for external
files.
If specified inline this is a nested array, the outer array defining the
polygon, the next array the rings and the innermost arrays the
coordinates.
This format is the same as in GeoJSON files.
.PP
In this example there is only one outer ring:
.IP
.EX
\(dqpolygon\(dq: [[
    [9.613465, 53.58071],
    [9.647599, 53.59655],
    [9.649288, 53.61059],
    [9.613465, 53.58071]
]]
.EE
.PP
In each ring, the last set of coordinates should be the same as the
first set, closing the ring.
.PP
\f[B]multipolygon\f[R]:
.PP
A multipolygon, either specified inline in the config file or read from
an external file.
See the \f[B](MULTI)POLYGON FILE FORMATS\f[R] section for external
files.
If specified inline this is a nested array, the outer array defining the
multipolygon, the next array the polygons, the next the rings and the
innermost arrays the coordinates.
This format is the same as in GeoJSON files.
.PP
In this example there is one outer and one inner ring:
.IP
.EX
\(dqmultipolygon\(dq: [[[
    [6.847, 50.987],
    [6.910, 51.007],
    [7.037, 50.953],
    [6.967, 50.880],
    [6.842, 50.925],
    [6.847, 50.987]
],[
    [6.967, 50.954],
    [6.969, 50.920],
    [6.932, 50.928],
    [6.934, 50.950],
    [6.967, 50.954]
]]]
.EE
.PP
In each ring, the last set of coordinates should be the same as the
first set, closing the ring.
.PP
Osmium must check each and every node in the input data and find out in
which bounding boxes or (multi)polygons this node is.
This is very cheap for bounding boxes, but more expensive for
(multi)polygons.
And it becomes more expensive the more vertices the (multi)polyon has.
Use bounding boxes or simplified polygons where possible.
.PP
Note that bounding boxes or (multi)polygons are not allowed to span the
\-180/180 degree line.
If you need this, cut out the regions on each side and use \f[B]osmium
merge\f[R] to join the resulting files.
.SH (MULTI)POLYGON FILE FORMATS
External files describing a (multi)polygon are specified in the config
file using the \(lqfile_name\(rq and \(lqfile_type\(rq properties on the
\(lqpolygon\(rq or \(lqmultipolygon\(rq object:
.IP
.EX
\(dqpolygon\(dq: {
    \(dqfile_name\(dq: \(dqberlin.geojson\(dq,
    \(dqfile_type\(dq: \(dqgeojson\(dq
}
.EE
.PP
If file names don\(cqt start with a slash (/), they are interpreted
relative to the directory where the config file is.
If the \(lqfile_type\(rq is missing, Osmium will try to autodetect it
from the suffix of the \(lqfile_name\(rq.
.PP
The following file types are supported:
.TP
geojson
GeoJSON file containing exactly one Feature of type Polygon or
MultiPolygon, or a FeatureCollection with the first Feature of type
Polygon or MultiPolygon.
Everything except the actual geometry (of the first Feature) is ignored.
.TP
poly
A poly file as described in
https://wiki.openstreetmap.org/wiki/Osmosis/Polygon_Filter_File_Format .
This wiki page also mentions several sources for such poly files.
.TP
osm
An OSM file containing one or more multipolygon or boundary relation
together with all the nodes and ways needed.
Any OSM file format (XML, PBF, \&...)
supported by Osmium can be used here, but the correct suffix must be
used, so the file format is detected correctly.
Files for this can easily be obtained by searching for the area on OSM
and then downloading the full relation using a URL like
https://www.openstreetmap.org/api/0.6/relation/RELATION\-ID/full .
Or you can use \f[B]osmium getid \-r\f[R] to get a specific relation
from an OSM file.
Note that both these approaches can get you very detailed boundaries
which can take quite a while to cut out.
Consider simplifying the boundary before use.
.PP
If there are several (multi)polygons in a poly file or OSM file, they
will be merged.
The (multi)polygons must not overlap, otherwise the result is undefined.
.SH STRATEGIES
\f[B]osmium extract\f[R] can use different strategies for creating the
extracts.
Depending on the strategy different objects will end up in the extracts.
The strategies differ in how much memory they need and how often they
need to read the input file.
The choice of strategy depends on how you want to use the generated
extracts and how much memory and time you have.
.PP
The default strategy is \f[B]complete_ways\f[R].
.TP
Strategy \f[B]simple\f[R]
Runs in a single pass.
The extract will contain all nodes inside the region and all ways
referencing those nodes as well as all relations referencing any nodes
or ways already included.
Ways crossing the region boundary will not be reference\-complete.
Relations will not be reference\-complete.
This strategy is fast, because it reads the input only once, but the
result is not enough for most use cases.
It is the only strategy that will work when reading from a socket or
pipe.
This strategy will not work for history files.
.TP
Strategy \f[B]complete_ways\f[R]
Runs in two passes.
The extract will contain all nodes inside the region and all ways
referencing those nodes as well as all nodes referenced by those ways.
The extract will also contain all relations referenced by nodes inside
the region or ways already included and, recursively, their parent
relations.
The ways are reference\-complete, but the relations are not.
.TP
Strategy \f[B]smart\f[R]
Runs in three passes.
The extract will contain all nodes inside the region and all ways
referencing those nodes as well as all nodes referenced by those ways.
The extract will also contain all relations referenced by nodes inside
the region or ways already included and, recursively, their parent
relations.
The extract will also contain all nodes and ways (and the nodes they
reference) referenced by relations tagged \(lqtype=multipolygon\(rq
directly referencing any nodes in the region or ways referencing nodes
in the region.
The ways are reference\-complete, and all multipolygon relations
referencing nodes in the regions or ways that have nodes in the region
are reference\-complete.
Other relations are not reference\-complete.
.PP
For the \f[B]complete_ways\f[R] strategy you can set the option \(lq\-S
relations=false\(rq in which case no relations will be written to the
output file.
.PP
The \f[B]smart\f[R] strategy allows the following strategy options:
.PP
Use \(lq\-S types=TYPE,\&...\(rq to change the types of relations that
should be reference\-complete.
Instead of just relations tagged \(lqtype=multipolygon\(rq, you can
either get all relations (use \(lq\-S types=any\(rq) or give a list of
types to the \-S option: \(lq\-S types=multipolygon,route\(rq.
Note that especially boundary relations can be huge, so if you include
them, be aware your result might be huge.
.PP
Use \(lq\-S complete\-partial\-relations=X\(rq to force completion of
partly completed relations.
If this is set, all relations that have more than X percent of their
members already in the extract will have their full set of members in
the extract.
So this allows completing almost complete relations.
It can be useful for instance to make sure a boundary relation is
complete even if some of it is outside the polygon used for extraction.
.PP
Use \(lq\-S tags=PATTERN,\&...\(rq to only complete relations that have
a tag matching one of the PATTERNs.
So for example if you use \(lq\-S
tags=landuse,natural=wood,natural=water\(rq everything tagged
\f[CR]landuse=*\f[R] or \f[CR]natural=wood\f[R] or
\f[CR]natural=water\f[R] is added to the result, but no other relations.
.PP
You can combine the \(lq\-S types\(rq, \(lq\-S
complete\-partial\-relations\(rq, and \(lq\-S tags\(rq options.
The options will be interpreted as \(lq(types OR
complete\-partial\-relations) AND tags\(rq.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
\f[B]osmium extract\f[R] exits with exit code
.TP
0
if everything went alright,
.TP
1
if there was an error processing the data, or
.TP
2
if there was a problem with the command line arguments, config file or
polygon files.
.SH MEMORY USAGE
Memory usage of \f[B]osmium extract\f[R] depends on the number of
extracts and on the strategy used.
For the \f[I]simple\f[R] strategy it will at least be the number of
extracts times the highest node ID used divided by 8.
For the \f[I]complete_ways\f[R] twice that and for the \f[I]smart\f[R]
strategy a bit more.
.PP
If you want to split a large file into many extracts, do this in several
steps.
First create several larger extracts and then split them again and again
into smaller pieces.
.SH LIMITS
You can not have more than 500 extracts.
Although chances are that you will be running out of memory long before
that.
See MEMORY USAGE.
.SH EXAMPLES
See the example config files in the \f[I]extract\-example\-config\f[R]
directory.
To try it:
.IP
.EX
osmium extract \-v \-c extract\-example\-config/extracts.json \(rs
    germany\-latest.osm.pbf
.EE
.PP
Extract the city of Karlsruhe using a boundary polygon:
.IP
.EX
osmium extract \-p karlsruhe\-boundary.osm.bz2 germany\-latest.osm.pbf \(rs
    \-o karlsruhe.osm.pbf
.EE
.PP
Extract the city of Munich using a bounding box:
.IP
.EX
osmium extract \-b 11.35,48.05,11.73,48.25 germany\-latest.osm.pbf \(rs
    \-o munich.osm.pbf
.EE
